l":{"explanation":"The only real difficulty is cycles: naively recursing into neighbours revisits the node you started\nfrom forever.\n\nKeep a map from original node to its copy, and create the copy **before** recursing. Then a\nneighbour that has already been cloned is found in the map instead of being cloned again, and the\nrecursion terminates.\n\n`O(V + E)` time and space. BFS with the same map works identically.","code":{"python":"def cloneGraph(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return None\n    copies = {node: Node(node.val)}\n    stack = [node]\n    while stack:\n        current = stack.pop()\n        for neighbour in current.neighbors:\n            if neighbour not in copies:\n                copies[neighbour] = Node(neighbour.val)\n                stack.append(neighbour)\n            copies[current].neighbors.append(copies[neighbour])\n    return copies[node]\n"},"complexity":"O(n + e) time, O(n) space"}}
